afk: subagents always exist here; no manual worktrees; tasks close as jj
changes; the final review dispatches the `reviewer` agent.

Motivation: upstream treats subagent support as optional and
platform-dependent. omp always provides the task tool, so inline
execution is opt-in only; workspace isolation is native (isolated
subagents), never a worktree skill. Upstream v6.4.1 rewrote this skill
as a full inline executor ("Native" at the writing-plans handoff) built
on per-task git commits: `task-start` records BASE, `task-done` ledgers
BASE..HEAD, and `review-package` now REFUSES an empty range. Our
writing-plans patch strips commit steps from plans (SDD lands tasks by
cherry-picking parked refs), and in a jj-colocated repo git HEAD only
moves when a jj change is closed — so without an explicit close step an
inline run leaves HEAD at BASE and every range above comes out empty.
The task tool has no model parameter (agent types instead), so "specify
the most capable model" does not map.

What must survive a rebase:
- "When to Use": the upstream "harness has no subagent tool" bullet is
  replaced by the statement that subagents always exist here, SDD is the
  default, and this skill runs only when the human partner explicitly
  chose inline execution.
- Each task closes as its own described change with
  `jj commit -m "Task <N>: ..."` (never `git commit`), replacing
  upstream's "Commit as the plan's commit steps say" in both the process
  graph and Step 2, with the reason (HEAD moves only then; task-done's
  range and the review package depend on it). Example workflow mirrors it.
- Final review dispatches the `reviewer` agent; the "specify the model
  explicitly" sentences are dropped.
- Dropped on purpose: the isolated-workspace paragraph in Setup (and
  "worktree" in the Setup graph node / example), and the "never start on
  main/master" line (jj flow, no branch checkouts).

